WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:05.580
Film Courage: What are some common mistakes
that are not so much the content of the screenplay

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.730
but things that people should be aware of
when they’re submitting?

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.520
Gordy&nbsp;Hoffman, Founder/Judge of the Bluecat
Screenplay Competition: Don’t submit it

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:18.589
until you’re ready. It’s like don’t
submit if you’re not ready and a lot of

00:00:18.589 --> 00:00:23.720
people want to be ready. We’ve talked about
being impatient, wanting the fight to end

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:29.150
(the fight for a great movie to end) and they’re
like “Ahhh, I’m going to just submit it.”

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:34.850
And then they’re bummed out that the feedback
comes back and their like “Oh? They didn’t

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:35.850
respond…Ugh.”

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:39.399
Work hard on your script and if you’re not
ready to submit to BlueCat, if you don’t

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:44.160
think it’s your best work, don’t submit
it. Don’t submit it. Just wait three months

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.070
and submit to Nicholl’s, submit to Austin,
submit to Sundance, submit to something else.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.539
Submit to the local state regional contest
that’s six months from now when your script

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:58.769
is really good and you’re ready to do it
and it’s like beautiful and you’re really

00:00:58.769 --> 00:01:00.179
excited about it.

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:04.780
But if you’re not ready, don’t submit.
That’s the thing where people send in stuff

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:09.750
where they’re not intimate with the material,
they don’t know. You can tell that they

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.380
haven’t gone back through it, that they
haven’t re-read it, that it’s overwritten,

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:18.610
that it’s too long and they’re sort of
like “Well, I’m done…you look at it.”

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:24.720
It’s like well we have a response where
we’re not connected to it because it’s

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:31.440
confusing or it’s not clear or whatever.
And that’s the thing that I think most people…again

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:39.400
you’re in a rush to finish and so you want
the adrenaline of “I’ve just entered a

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:46.090
competition. I’m making progress.” You
know it’s sort of like stay with the script

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:51.010
and be patient and make sure that you know
your script. Be intimate with your script.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:56.170
I mean the scripts that always come out on
top with BlueCat after all these years (we

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:02.020
get a lot more submissions) they are always
scripts that are really tight as a drum. There

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:08.070
are people who have clearly worked really
hard. Sometimes people write a draft and they

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:15.910
proofread it and they send it in and it’s
like “that was my second draft of whatever.”&nbsp;

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:20.860
Generally you can tell people have worked
really hard and they know everything in their

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:26.000
script like the back of their hand and I think
that’s the biggest thing I would &nbsp; encourage

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.870
people, you should know your script really
well and if you know your script really well

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:38.790
and you know if has problems or it doesn’t
and if you know it really well you know that

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.530
if it does have problems that you shouldn’t
be submitting it to a competition. Unless

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:46.030
you’re just like &nbsp;I just want Bluecat’s
feedback or whatever and a lot of people just

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:49.250
do that and they’re just like I just want
to get some notes back.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:54.470
Film Courage: Would you say when you were
writing LOVE LIZA that you were obsessed with

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:59.090
it? In that you were so in that world, where
you weren’t working and you saw that one

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:09.200
woman [at the gas station which inspired his
script], do you want that same obsession like

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.200
this is everything that you see on this piece
of paper.&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:17.200
Gordy: Well yeah I think most people who have
written something special that has done really

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:25.540
well they love it. The thing about screenwriting
and going back to my mission with Bluecat

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:34.720
and the place of Bluecat in my life and the
meaning is that everybody we deal with is

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:43.040
doing what they want to do, it’s their dream,
it’s their passion, it’s really important

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.069
to them. So I don’t think that people are
like “Well I’m doing this screenwriting

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:54.330
thing but I really, really like being a stockbroker.
That’s what I really like doing but I’

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:58.980
just working on screenplays because I don’t
know…I’m just doing it because I’ve

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:06.069
got to do it. My Mom wanted me to do it but
I really want to sell real estate.” It’s

00:04:06.069 --> 00:04:14.930
not like that. So everybody we deal with it’s
important to them. Everybody that I talk to

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:24.550
is invested so there’s not really an issue
of obsession, investment, priority, with everybody

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:28.360
entering Bluecat. Everybody entering Bluecat
is thrilled and excited. They are not like

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:37.210
“Oh? I won. But I really wanted open that
paint store and that didn’t go through and

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:41.009
that’s what I really wanted. And I won this
screenwriting award instead.”

00:04:41.009 --> 00:04:46.949
Film Courage: That being said, you say people
rush to get their screenplay in. You can just

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:52.100
tell that they haven’t invested enough,
so then what is that disconnected there? You

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:56.479
can tell that this is what they want to be
doing but they submit something (sometimes)

00:04:56.479 --> 00:04:58.069
that isn’t ready?

00:04:58.069 --> 00:05:09.210
Gordy: You have to learn because when you’re
a beginning writer and you ignore the feedback

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:13.479
you’re trying to give yourself but you’re
like “No but I want it to done. And it’s

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:18.009
done! Let’s go shoot it.” And then you
don’t get the response you’re like “Oh

00:05:18.009 --> 00:05:22.409
I should have kept rewriting it. I knew it
wasn’t good. I knew I had problems with

00:05:22.409 --> 00:05:27.020
it. But I submitted it to Bluecat anyways
and then I got feedback and clearly it’s

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:28.150
not ready yet.”

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:35.620
And then the seasons writer, the more developed
writer, the one who is more mature is like

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:41.860
“I’m not going to submit that because
it’s not ready.” Then I put my best foot

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:42.860
forward.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:48.129
Film Courage: How do you monitor yourself
in terms of wanting to put something out there

00:05:48.129 --> 00:05:51.539
and knowing “Okay, I know myself. This isn’t
ready yet?”

00:05:51.539 --> 00:05:59.410
Gordy: I know myself like that it’s not
good enough and then it’s or sometimes it’s

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:03.199
like I want to get feedback so I’ll have
a table read and I’ll have somebody read

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:07.960
it and I’ll be like “What do you think?”
And they want some feedback but you’re definitely

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:13.470
not going to send it off to the producer or
the actress that is attached to your project

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:23.639
until you’ve done a lot more. So there are
certain stages where you already know that

00:06:23.639 --> 00:06:30.069
this is broken. You don’t need to send this
out again. You already have given yourself

00:06:30.069 --> 00:06:35.520
notes. We give ourselves notes, then we can
get notes from other people then we can movie

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:42.559
it up the food chain but generally it’s
all about being honest and transparent with

00:06:42.559 --> 00:06:49.419
ourselves and knowing that. But you do learn
that in sort of the early days when you’re

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:54.369
like “Well I’m just going to see…I want
somebody to read this.” And then you’re

00:06:54.369 --> 00:06:57.819
like “Okay, It think I’m going to wait
next time until somebody reads this because

00:06:57.819 --> 00:07:02.619
that wasn’t too pleasant. They didn’t
really respond and by the way (P.S.) I knew

00:07:02.619 --> 00:07:07.710
it wasn’t ready but I sent it to them anyways
because I just wanted somebody to read it.”

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.849
You just want somebody to read it because
it makes it real when someone else reads it

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:16.729
“I wrote a screenplay.” But after you
sort of get over the novelty of that then

00:07:16.729 --> 00:07:19.259
you start to want to write better work.

